<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="D-serine degradation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY0-1535" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; D-Amino acids are synthesized by amino acid racemases. In bacteria D-serine is produced by the general-specificity |FRAME:EC-5.1.1.18 |. Eukaryotes have a serine-specific enzyme, |FRAME: EC-5.1.1.10|, |CITS: [19249065]| (see |FRAME: PWY-8140|). Many bacterial species, such as |FRAME:TAX-562|, are able to utilize |FRAME: D-SERINE| as the sole source of arbon and nitrogen for growth |CITS: [1090588]|. D-serine also has a bacteriostatic effect due to its effect on the biosynthesis of L-serine and pantothenate |CITS: [4574697]|. Detoxification of D-serine and its utilization as a carbon and nitrogen source are accomplished by a single enzyme, |FRAME: EC-4.3.1.18| |CITS: [14216615]|. D-serine ammonia-lyase, which is induced by D-serine, catalyzes the deamination of D-serine to form pyruvate and ammonia |CITS: [13271312][14216615][14216616]|. Recently, it has been shown that the ability to utilize and/or detoxify D-serine plays a role in pathogenesis by certain pathogenic bacteria |CITS: [12823810][17088369][17785472][19674114]|. The vertebrate brain contains significant amounts of |FRAME: D-SERINE| which has a role in &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-methyl-D-aspartate receptor function. In the mammalian brain |FRAME: D-SERINE|, produced from |FRAME: SER| by a bifunctional serine racemase/D-serine dehydratase, is mainly degraded by |FRAME: EC-1.4.3.3|. Plants, like mammals, have a bifunctional serine racemase/D-serine dehydratase |CITS: [16483618]|. In the avian brain D-amino acid oxidase is not expressed and |FRAME: D-SERINE| is degraded by |FRAME: EC-4.3.1.18|. In other avian tissues both enzymes are present |CITS: [17254537][21676877]|. D-serine dehydratase has also been identified in yeast |CITS: [17937657]|.</Description>
